WHAT
    Give masm's OSAllocator a wasi implementation, backed by
    aligned_alloc(pageSize()) rather than by wasi-libc's emulated mmap.

WHY -- part 1, the link error
    masm's Platform.h decides OS(UNIX) from a fixed list of system names plus
    `defined(unix)`, and wasi-sdk defines none of them, so the whole of
    OSAllocatorPosix.cpp compiles to nothing. src/qml/CMakeLists.txt:721 DOES
    add the file (`CONDITION UNIX AND NOT INTEGRITY`, which qtbase's WASI patch
    satisfies); it is the in-file guard that drops it. QtQml then does not link:

        wasm-ld: error: libQt6Qml.a(qv4mm.cpp.obj): undefined symbol:
            WTF::OSAllocator::commit(void*, unsigned long, bool, bool)
        wasm-ld: error: libQt6Qml.a(YarrInterpreter.cpp.obj): undefined symbol:
            WTF::OSAllocator::reserveAndCommit(...)
        wasm-ld: error: libQt6Qml.a(qv4executableallocator.cpp.obj): undefined
            symbol: WTF::OSAllocator::releaseDecommitted(void*, unsigned long)

    -- the JS heap, the regexp interpreter's stack, and the executable
    allocator.

WHY -- part 2, why widening the guard is NOT enough
    Simply letting the POSIX file compile does link, and then crashes. Every
    OS(LINUX)/OS(QNX)/OS(DARWIN) branch in it is false for us and
    HAVE(MADV_FREE)/HAVE(MADV_FREE_REUSE) are set only for DARWIN and QNX
    (Platform.h:673-701), so the generic paths use only mmap and munmap, both
    of which wasi-libc's libwasi-emulated-mman provides. But wasi-libc
    implements mmap as malloc and munmap as free, and malloc is 8-byte
    aligned where mmap is PAGE aligned. Measured under wasmtime:

        sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE) = 65536
        mmap(65536) = 0x11498   page-aligned=0
        mmap(65536) = 0x214b8   page-aligned=0

    qtdeclarative relies on that alignment. src/qml/jsruntime/qv4persistent.cpp
    puts one WTF::PageAllocation behind every page of PersistentValues and
    recovers a page's header from any Value inside it by MASKING:

        Page *getPage(const Value *val) {
           return reinterpret_cast<Page *>(reinterpret_cast<quintptr>(val)
                   & ~((quintptr)(WTF::pageSize() - 1)));
        }

    With an unaligned page that mask lands on an unrelated address and every
    header field read through it is garbage. allocatePage() asserts exactly
    this -- Q_ASSERT(!((quintptr)p & (WTF::pageSize() - 1))) -- so a debug
    build would say so; a release build silently corrupts the GC's bookkeeping.
    Observed, in Slate, after QML had loaded and bindings were evaluating:

        0: QV4::markPersistentValues(QV4::GCStateMachine*, ...)
        2: QV4::GCStateMachine::transition()
        3: QV4::MemoryManager::runGC()
        ...
        memory fault at wasm address 0xffff00fc in linear memory of size
        0x2d80000: wasm trap: out of bounds memory access

    aligned_alloc(65536, ...) does give real 64 KiB alignment on wasi-libc's
    dlmalloc (verified under wasmtime: 0x20000, 0x40000, 0x60000), and free()
    takes exactly that pointer, which is all releaseDecommitted has to do.
    The rest follows from wasm having no virtual memory: reserveUncommitted IS
    reserveAndCommit and commit()/decommit() are no-ops -- the same shape the
    generic POSIX path already takes. The memset is not optional: qv4mm.cpp
    assumes a fresh reservation is zeroed (MemorySegment::free() zeroes before
    decommit precisely because of that), mmap would have zeroed, malloc does
    not.

    canAllocateExecutableMemory() returns a constant instead of probing.
    wasm code is not data; there is no way to make linear memory executable.
    But wasi-libc's mprotect() is a stub that returns 0, so upstream's probe
    -- mmap RW, then mprotect PROT_EXEC -- would report SUCCESS.

UPSTREAM
    Not offered: masm is a vendored fork of an old JavaScriptCore. The
    alignment lesson is more general, and is worth remembering for any wasi
    port: wasi-libc's mmap is malloc, so ANY code that masks a pointer back to
    a page boundary is broken on it.

CLASSIFICATION
    as-is for the allocator: same contract, different primitive.
    The canAllocateExecutableMemory() hunk deliberately replaces a runtime
    probe with a constant, because the probe cannot answer truthfully here.
